<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret of York</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1446">1446</a>–<a href="/years/1503">1503</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Margaret, Duchess of Burgundy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret of York was a sister of the English kings Edward IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, in 1468. After his death she supported her stepdaughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-of-burgundy">Mary of Burgundy</a> and her step-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip the Fair</a>, and held court at Mechelen, where she was a notable patron of books and art. She also backed Yorkist pretenders against Henry VII.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-of-york.jpg" alt="Margaret of York" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous / Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Margaret_of_York.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> was born at Fotheringhay Castle on 3 May 1446, the daughter of Richard of York, 3rd Duke of York, and Cecily Neville, and the sister of Edward IV and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a>. When her father held power in 1453-1454, marriage talks with the Burgundian court proposed her as a wife for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a>, but they petered out. Her father was killed at the Battle of Wakefield on 30 December 1460, and by 1465 her brother was king, which made her a far more valuable bride. When Charles's wife Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> died in September 1465, he sent an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to propose the match.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The marriage took years to arrange. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a> opposed an alliance between his two main enemies, and in 1466 Margaret was briefly betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-constable-of-portugal">Peter, Constable of Portugal</a>, who died on 29 June. Edward IV agreed formally in October 1467, the contract was completed in February 1468, and Margaret crossed from Margate to Sluys on 23 June 1468. She and Charles were married privately at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damme">Damme</a> on 3 July. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> celebrations, including the Tournament of the Golden Tree, impressed observers as the marriage of the century. She presented her crown in 1475 to the Church of Our Lady at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>, where it survives.</p><p>The marriage produced no children, but Margaret became a mother figure to her stepdaughter Mary. After Charles was killed outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a> on 5 January 1477, she advised Mary to marry Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and they wed on 18 August. Mary died after a riding accident on 27 March 1482. In December 1482 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-arras-1482">Treaty of Arras</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> the Burgundian Lowlands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a>. Margaret later governed with the Burgundian Estates and guarded Mary's son Philip, and her residence at Mechelen became a princely court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> was killed at Bosworth in 1485, Margaret supported challengers to Henry VII, backing Lambert Simnel and Perkin Warbeck, whom she acknowledged as her nephew and funded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> in 1495. Warbeck was defeated at Deal and failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>. She died on 23 November 1503, aged 57, and was buried at the Church of the Cordeliers in Mechelen.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-caxton">William Caxton</a> counted her among his patrons, and a presentation copy of his Recuyell of the Historyes of Troye shows him offering it to her. She also commissioned illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, among them The Visions of Tondal, illustrated by Simon Marmion. Her actions are credited with the survival of the Burgundian state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret of York” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Margaret of York</b> and then Margaret of Austria, regent for Charles V, kept their courts there, making it a Renaissance centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mechelen">Lordship of Mechelen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fotheringhay Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Mechelen, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.52,-0.44&amp;year=1483" aria-label="Fotheringhay Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Margaret of York"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4977 5637h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4977 5637h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.52,-0.44&amp;year=1483">What was here in 1483? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
